  "texto": "Move four. The debt she never owed. 'After everything I've done for you.' He said it after the trip. After the dinner. After the night he drove her home. Every kindness became a receipt. And Maya was always in debt. Money became part of it too. Small things first. 'Let me handle the bills. You'd only stress.' Soon, she needed permission to spend her own paycheck. And it never once looked like control. She tried to pay it back. She cooked. She apologized. She changed how she dressed. And every time she got close to being 'good enough,' the rules quietly changed. Yesterday, the problem was that she was too quiet. Today, she was too loud. There was no version of Maya that could win, because winning was never the game. Guilt turns love into leverage. Moving goalposts means you never finish paying. Here's the part nobody sees: the target isn't obedience. It's exhaustion. A tired person doesn't argue. A tired person just tries harder. But the fifth move is the one that keeps people for years. Because it's the only one that feels like hope.",
  "voz": "en-US-AndrewNeural",
  "rate": "-4%",
